Negative control for the FE-3113 arm-barrier spec (`AC3`).

`armCorpusModule` is the barrier: when it reports true, the module it names must
already be the corpus a handler list is built from. This mutant keeps the load —
the recordings still arrive, a beat later — and only moves the report ahead of
it, which is exactly the un-awaited `void armCorpusModule(...)` shape the story
found on the page. Nothing looks withdrawn: the module resolves, the promise
resolves true, and a moment afterwards the corpus is there.

What must go red is the ORDER, not the outcome. An assertion that re-reads the
corpus after another tick, or that only checks the boolean, passes the mutant and
proves nothing — the page reports armed, the worker registers zero handlers, and
every request goes past it to STAGING under a chip that says replaying. Read the
shared pointer SYNCHRONOUSLY after the await resolves.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

Reds: the arm-barrier spec — armed means installed, for every module force can
reach.

